To create this range query, you can modify the end of the "Daily" and "Weekly" cases like this:

```kotlin
when (timeFrame) {
    "Daily" -> {
        // Daily: Showing 7 days in a week
        ...

        // Add this at the end of the "Daily" case
        val startDocId = dateFormat.format(startCalendar.time)
        val endDocId = dateFormat.format(endCalendar.time)
        query = query.orderBy(FieldPath.documentId()).startAt(startDocId).endAt(endDocId)
        ...
    }

    "Weekly" -> {
        // Weekly: Showing 4 weeks in a month
        ...

        // Add this at the end of the "Weekly" case
        val startDocId = dateFormat.format(startCalendar.time)
        val endDocId = dateFormat.format(endCalendar.time)
        query = query.orderBy(FieldPath.documentId()).startAt(startDocId).endAt(endDocId)
        ...
    }
    ...
}
```

This will order the documents by their document IDs and fetch only the relevant documents in the specified range based on the start and end document IDs.

One thing to note with this approach is that the document IDs should be ordered lexicographically. Since you're creating your document IDs in the format "Saturday_22_Apr_2023", it looks like this assumption holds. However, I would suggest using a more standard date format like "yyyy_MM_dd" for the document IDs, so ordering them lexicographically gives you the correct date order.

This should help you fetch the relevant data between the start and end dates for each time frame based on the document IDs.
